      "text": "They are actual stars… the fulness of light, in which the morning stars shone forth all together at the founding of the earth, may symbolize one grandly harmonious song of joy.",
      "attr": "Keil & Delitzsch (Delitzsch d. 1890), Biblical Commentary on the OT",
      "voice": null,
      "href": null,
      "source_kind": "hand",
      "source_code": null,
      "ord": 0,
      "id": 3
    },
    {
      "contradiction_id": 3,
      "pole": "discrepancy",
      "text": "The stars generally, or the actual stars visible on the morn of creation, are probably meant… Their priority to the earth is implied, since they witness its birth.",
      "attr": "The Pulpit Commentary (1880s)",
      "voice": null,
      "href": null,
      "source_kind": "hand",
      "source_code": null,
      "ord": 1,
      "id": 4
    },
    {
      "contradiction_id": 4,
      "pole": "reconcile",
      "text": "Many have asked, “How could light be produced on the first day, and the sun, the fountain of it, not created till the fourth day?”… I therefore conclude, that as God has diffused the matter of caloric or latent heat through every part of nature, without which there could be neither vegetation nor animal life, that it is caloric or latent heat which is principally intended by the original word.",
      "attr": "Adam Clarke (1832), Commentary on the Bible, on Genesis 1:3.",
      "voice": null,
      "href": null,
      "source_kind": "machine",
      "source_code": "CLARKE",
      "ord": 0,
      "id": 5
    },
    {
      "contradiction_id": 4,
      "pole": "reconcile",
      "text": "the body of fire and light produced on the first day was now distributed and formed into several luminous bodies of sun, moon, and stars, for these were “from light”; lights produced from that light, or made out of it… as the light by its circular motion did for the first three days, or the diurnal motion of the earth on its axis, then and now…",
      "attr": "John Gill (1746-1763), Exposition of the Entire Bible, on Genesis 1:14.",
